{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788201833,"nonce":"vtOv6EsGb1eILuZYN+w5xHIwEHc=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCONFIRMED — the diagnosis is exactly right, member for member. But the AUDIT LIST\nIS WRONG BY OMISSION, and following it literally would close this issue with the\nsame bug still live in a second step. That is the one thing to fix before\ndispatch.\n\nWHAT I CONFIRMED BY READING (all in `Source/AutoRimmer/WorldFixtureVerbs.cs`)\n\n- `AddBill(Map, VerbContext, Dictionary\u003cstring,object\u003e)` takes no bench handle and\n  opens with `var giver = FindBench(map, ctx) as IBillGiver;`. `Bench(...)`\n  returns only a `string` label; the spawned `Thing` is never stored anywhere a\n  later step can reach. The two steps are dispatched from the same\n  `foreach (var step in steps)` switch with nothing shared between them.\n  CONFIRMED.\n- `FindBench` walks `map.listerThings.ThingsInGroup(ThingRequestGroup.PotentialBillGiver)`\n  and returns the first player-faction `TableButcher`. `Verse/ListerThings.Add`\n  APPENDS to the per-group list, so \"first\" is \"oldest spawned\" and the second\n  `bench` step lands behind the first. CONFIRMED, and that is the whole\n  mechanism.\n- The error string is verbatim as quoted: `\"bill needs a bill giver (run the\n  `bench` step first)\"` — the verb does assert the chaining it does not perform.\n  CONFIRMED.\n- `Bench` publishes `[\"expect_bills\"] = 0` as a literal, with no read of the\n  stack. CONFIRMED.\n- `accept/32b9e01-orders-makingfor.ps1` contains `New-FixtureBench` and the\n  `Precondition \"$Num.d\"` that asserts `[int]$billBench -eq [int]$id`, i.e. the\n  workaround and its guard are both real and already written. CONFIRMED.\n\nTHE OMISSION — `open-letter` HAS THE SAME DEFECT AND IS NOT IN THE AUDIT LIST\n\n`WorldFixtureVerbs.OpenLetter(Dictionary\u003cstring,object\u003e extras)`:\n\n    foreach (var l in stack)\n        if (l != null \u0026\u0026 l.Label.ToString().StartsWith(FixtureLetterLabel, StringComparison.Ordinal))\n        { found = l; break; }\n    if (found == null) throw new VerbArgsException(\"no fixture letter in the stack (run the `letter` step first)\");\n\nAll three tells of the `bill` bug, together: first-match over a shared live list,\nan error string that ASSERTS the chaining, and an id published by the earlier\nstep (`extras[\"letter\"][\"id\"]`) that is never consulted. Two `letter` steps in a\nsession and `open-letter` opens the older one.\n\nThe issue's audit sentence — \"`stockpiles`, `growing`, `research` and `letter` all\nresolve their targets independently\" — names four steps that CREATE things and\ntherefore have nothing to chain to, and omits the one step that resolves. A\nworker who audits exactly those four will correctly report \"none of these can\nchain\" and close the class while it is still open. That is not a hypothetical\nreading; it is the most natural one, because the sentence reads as an exhaustive\nlist.\n\nRecommendation: replace that clause with \"every step in the `Fixture` switch\",\nand name `open-letter` explicitly in the acceptance so it cannot be missed. The\nfix for it is identical to `bill`'s and costs almost nothing once `bill` is done\n— which is exactly why it should not be deferred to a second round trip.\n\nTHREE MORE THINGS IN THE SAME FILE, SAME FAMILY, NONE IN THE ISSUE\n\n1. `expect_slots_free` is the twin of `expect_bills` and is also a literal\n   (`[\"expect_slots_free\"] = 15`). The acceptance names only `expect_bills`, so\n   an implementation can satisfy the acceptance to the letter and still report\n   15 free slots on a bench holding two bills. Fix both or the fix is half done.\n2. `AddBill` adds TWO bills unconditionally and never clears, and does not check\n   `BillStack.MaxCount` (which is 15, and `RimWorld/BillStack.AddBill` validates\n   nothing — it is `bill.billStack = this; bills.Add(bill);` and nothing else).\n   Chaining fixes WHICH bench; it does not fix ACCUMULATION on repeat. Eight\n   `bill` steps against one bench push the stack past the cap that the game's own\n   UI enforces and the model does not. Worth a decision: does the `bill` step\n   clear first, or refuse at the cap?\n3. `AddBill` constructs `new Bill_Production(chosen)` directly, bypassing\n   `RimWorld/BillUtility.MakeNewBill`, which dispatches to `Bill_ProductionWithUft`,\n   `Bill_ResurrectMech`, `Bill_ProductionMech` or `Bill_Autonomous` depending on\n   the recipe. Since the step accepts a caller-supplied `recipe` argument, a\n   recipe that uses an unfinished thing gets staged as the WRONG RUNTIME TYPE —\n   the same \"silently stages the wrong object\" class this issue is filed about,\n   one level down.\n\nA FOURTH, only loosely related but in the same file and worth knowing:\n`Fixture` iterates `steps` with no dedupe and every handler writes\n`extras[\"\u003ckey\u003e\"] = ...`, so `{steps:[\"bench\",\"bench\"]}` spawns two tables,\njournals two dev events, appends \"bench\" twice to `executed`, and reports only\nthe second. If the chaining fix introduces a handle, this is the natural moment\nto decide what a repeated step means.\n\nACCEPTANCE — THREE BULLETS TESTABLE, TWO NOT\n\n- \"Within ONE call, a later step operates on the object an earlier step created …\n  run TWICE leaves two benches with two bills each\" — testable on the bench, and\n  `bills` already reads both benches so the check mechanises cleanly.\n- \"`data.bill.bench_id` equals `data.bench.id` … and there is a test that would\n  fail if it did not\" — testable, and the test already exists as\n  `New-FixtureBench`'s `Precondition \"$Num.d\"`; lift it.\n- \"An explicit `bench` arg still wins\" — testable.\n- \"`data.bench.expect_bills` reflects the bills present when the call RETURNS *or*\n  the field is removed … with the choice recorded\" — **NOT TESTABLE AS WRITTEN.**\n  It is an unresolved either/or: a run where the field is absent and a run where\n  it reads `2` both pass, so no assertion can fail deterministically on a wrong\n  implementation. Pick one on-issue. (Implementation note if you keep it:\n  `extras[\"bench\"]` is built inside `Bench(...)` BEFORE `AddBill(...)` runs, so\n  keeping it means either mutating `extras` after the loop or deferring the block\n  — the acceptance names neither, and the second is cleaner.)\n- \"Audit the other steps … say in the closing comment which of them can and\n  cannot be chained\" — **NOT TESTABLE**, and actively misleading for the reason\n  above. Make it \"every step in the switch\", and it becomes a checkable\n  enumeration rather than a vibe.\n\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n\n- The live envelope (`bench.id 23492` / `bill.bench_id 23491`, four bills) is a\n  bench observation I did not reproduce. It is corroborated twice in-tree though\n  — `RUNLOG.md` and the comment block above `New-FixtureBench` in\n  `accept/32b9e01-orders-makingfor.ps1` both record the same two ids — so I would\n  treat it as established rather than as an unverified claim.\n- That the `MaxCount` overflow (point 2) has actually occurred. The mechanism is\n  read from the code; I have no observation of it.","files":null}]}